What already exists
1. ValidationRunner

This is the orchestration layer.

It already:

creates a ValidationRun;
retrieves registered rule sets;
executes the core validation stage;
executes the extension validation stage;
collects canonical ValidationResult instances;
invokes DecisionAggregator;
completes and freezes the run.

The flow is already essentially:

Subject
   ↓
ValidationRun created
   ↓
CoreValidator
   ↓
ExtensionValidator
   ↓
DecisionAggregator
   ↓
Completed ValidationRun

No redesign is required.

2. CoreValidator

CoreValidator is not a product validator containing hard-coded Atlas rules.

It is a generic rule executor for product-agnostic rule sets.

It:

selects rule sets without extension metadata;
executes every rule synchronously;
accepts boolean, structured-object, or ValidationResult outcomes;
normalizes them into canonical ValidationResult objects;
converts rule exceptions into blocking execution errors.

That is the correct abstraction.

3. ExtensionValidator

ExtensionValidator performs the corresponding role for extension rule sets.

It:

executes the rule sets passed to it;
does not contain RAM knowledge;
normalizes extension results;
preserves rule metadata;
safely converts exceptions into canonical error results.

Therefore, ExtensionValidator should remain generic. RAM logic belongs in the RAM extension files.

4. RuleRegistry

The registry already owns rule-set discovery and lifecycle.

It supports:

registration;
duplicate prevention;
immutable defensive copies;
retrieval by ID;
extension-based discovery;
deterministic insertion order.

The RAM extension will need to export a valid rule-set object that can be registered here.

